WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.203
Welcome to Thank You, Ma’am, adapted from a story by Langston Hughes.

00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:08.455
At about eleven o’clock at night, a boy tries to snatch a woman’s purse.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.531
Snatch means to grab suddenly.

00:00:10.751 --> 00:00:13.353
The strap breaks with one tug, a sharp pull.

00:00:13.573 --> 00:00:14.960
But the purse is heavy.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.833
Its weight and the boy’s weight make him lose his balance, and he falls on his back instead of running away.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:22.376
The woman catches him.

00:00:22.596 --> 00:00:28.847
Notice that the story begins with a wrong choice, but that is only the beginning of what we learn about the boy.

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:31.627
Why does the boy fail to escape with the purse?

00:00:39.127 --> 00:00:43.969
The strap breaks, but the combined weight of the boy and the purse throws him off balance.

00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:45.811
He falls on the sidewalk.

00:00:46.831 --> 00:00:50.841
The woman makes the boy pick up her purse and asks whether he is ashamed.

00:00:51.061 --> 00:00:52.896
He apologises in a whisper.

00:00:53.116 --> 00:00:56.444
He looks about fourteen or fifteen, frail and willow-wild.

00:00:56.664 --> 00:01:01.845
Frail means slight or weak-looking; willow-wild suggests a thin, restless appearance.

00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:05.628
She asks whether anyone at home tells him to wash his face.

00:01:05.848 --> 00:01:06.936
He says no.

00:01:07.156 --> 00:01:11.828
She takes him to her home, saying she will wash his face and teach him right from wrong.

00:01:12.048 --> 00:01:14.992
Her name is Mrs Luella Bates Washington Jones.

00:01:15.212 --> 00:01:16.833
The boy’s name is Roger.

00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:21.811
What does Mrs Jones do after catching Roger, and what two names should you remember?

00:01:29.311 --> 00:01:32.873
She makes him return the purse and takes him home to wash his face.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:37.317
The woman is Mrs Luella Bates Washington Jones; the boy is Roger.

00:01:38.337 --> 00:01:41.879
Inside her home, Mrs Jones turns Roger loose at the sink.

00:01:42.099 --> 00:01:45.875
She tells him to let the water run warm and gives him a clean towel.

00:01:46.095 --> 00:01:47.353
The door is open.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:50.624
Roger could dash away down the hall, but he does not.

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.167
He washes his face.

00:01:52.387 --> 00:01:58.595
When he asks whether she will take him to jail, she says she needs to cook supper and notices that he may be hungry.

00:01:58.815 --> 00:02:00.415
Supper means an evening meal.

00:02:00.635 --> 00:02:06.608
This scene matters because Roger now has a real choice: run from her, or stay and see what happens.

00:02:06.828 --> 00:02:10.775
What opportunity does Roger have at the open door, and what does he choose?

00:02:18.275 --> 00:02:20.323
He has the opportunity to run away.

00:02:20.543 --> 00:02:24.319
Instead, he stays and washes his face at Mrs Jones’s sink.

00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:29.669
Mrs Jones guesses that Roger may have been hungry enough to try stealing.

00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:32.876
He explains that he wanted a pair of blue suede shoes.

00:02:33.096 --> 00:02:35.464
Suede is soft leather with a brushed surface.

00:02:35.684 --> 00:02:39.161
The shoes are what he wanted, but they do not make stealing right.

00:02:39.381 --> 00:02:41.685
Mrs Jones tells him he could have asked her.

00:02:41.905 --> 00:02:43.121
He is surprised.

00:02:43.341 --> 00:02:52.323
The chapter invites us to hold two facts together: Roger made a harmful choice, and he is also a young person with a wish and a need for attention.

00:02:52.543 --> 00:02:56.575
What did Roger want, and how does Mrs Jones say he could have tried to get it?

00:03:04.075 --> 00:03:05.739
He wanted blue suede shoes.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:09.863
Mrs Jones says he could have asked her instead of trying to snatch her purse.

00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:15.832
Mrs Jones tells Roger that she was young once and wanted things she could not get.

00:03:16.052 --> 00:03:18.569
She says she has done things she would not tell him.

00:03:18.789 --> 00:03:23.291
She does not describe those things, and the story does not invite us to invent them.

00:03:23.511 --> 00:03:26.263
She is not pretending that wrongdoing is harmless.

00:03:26.483 --> 00:03:30.813
She recognises that people can make mistakes and still be treated as people.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:37.113
She asks Roger to sit while she makes food and suggests that he comb his hair so he will look presentable.

00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:39.957
Presentable means neat enough to meet others.

00:03:40.177 --> 00:03:44.807
What does Mrs Jones reveal about her younger self, and what does she leave unsaid?

00:03:52.307 --> 00:03:57.277
She says she too wanted things she could not get and has done things she will not tell Roger.

00:03:57.497 --> 00:04:00.612
The chapter does not specify what those actions were.

00:04:01.632 --> 00:04:04.213
Mrs Jones goes behind a screen to cook.

00:04:04.433 --> 00:04:07.825
She does not watch Roger or the purse she has left on the bed.

00:04:08.045 --> 00:04:13.528
Roger could take it, yet he moves to the far side of the room so that she could see him if she wished.

00:04:13.748 --> 00:04:17.289
The text tells us why: he does not want to be mistrusted now.

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:20.987
Mistrusted means thought to be dishonest or unreliable.

00:04:21.207 --> 00:04:25.111
The purse remains within reach, but Roger begins to act differently.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:28.616
Mrs Jones’s trust gives him room to show that change.

00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:32.356
Why does Roger sit where Mrs Jones can easily see him?

00:04:39.856 --> 00:04:41.797
He does not want her to mistrust him.

00:04:42.017 --> 00:04:46.796
Choosing to remain visible helps him show that he will not take the unattended purse.

00:04:47.816 --> 00:04:52.360
Mrs Jones heats lima beans and meat, makes cocoa, and sets the table.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:58.319
As they eat, she does not press Roger about his home or family—questions that might embarrass him.

00:04:58.539 --> 00:05:01.547
Embarrass means make someone feel awkward or ashamed.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:06.417
Instead, she talks about her work in a hotel beauty shop that stays open late.

00:05:06.637 --> 00:05:08.323
The meal is more than food.

00:05:08.543 --> 00:05:14.388
Roger receives ordinary care and conversation after a moment when he expected punishment.

00:05:14.608 --> 00:05:17.957
His choice to stay and her choice not to pry both matter.

00:05:18.177 --> 00:05:22.145
What does Mrs Jones serve, and what topic does she avoid during the meal?

00:05:29.645 --> 00:05:32.184
She serves lima beans, meat and cocoa.

00:05:32.404 --> 00:05:36.969
She avoids questions about Roger’s home and family that might embarrass him.

00:05:37.989 --> 00:05:42.491
After the meal, Mrs Jones gives Roger ten dollars for the blue suede shoes.

00:05:42.711 --> 00:05:46.295
She also warns him not to snatch her purse, or anyone else’s.

00:05:46.515 --> 00:05:52.680
She calls such stealing a devilish act—something seriously wrong that can bring painful consequences.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.545
Her gift does not excuse his attempt to steal.

00:05:55.765 --> 00:05:58.475
It shows that she believes he can choose a better way.

00:05:58.695 --> 00:06:04.561
The story never shows Roger spending the money, so remember only what the chapter actually tells us.

00:06:04.781 --> 00:06:08.323
What two things does Mrs Jones give Roger before he leaves?

00:06:15.823 --> 00:06:20.793
She gives him ten dollars to buy the shoes and a clear warning not to steal purses again.

00:06:21.813 --> 00:06:24.736
At the door, Mrs Jones wishes Roger goodnight.

00:06:24.956 --> 00:06:29.564
Roger wants to say something more than “Thank you, Ma’am,” but he cannot find the words.

00:06:29.784 --> 00:06:33.475
He barely manages that short thank-you before she shuts the door.

00:06:33.695 --> 00:06:35.295
Barely means only just.

00:06:35.515 --> 00:06:38.523
The chapter ends by saying he never sees her again.

00:06:38.743 --> 00:06:44.289
We may infer that the kindness affected him deeply, because he struggles to express what he feels.

00:06:44.509 --> 00:06:46.792
But we do not know what happens to him later.

00:06:47.012 --> 00:06:49.295
His silence gives the ending its force.

00:06:49.515 --> 00:06:53.312
What does Roger manage to say, and what does the story leave unknown?

00:07:00.812 --> 00:07:07.681
He manages to say “Thank you, Ma’am.” The story does not tell us what he wanted to add or what he did after he left.

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:10.728
Tell the story back in five steps.

00:07:10.948 --> 00:07:13.316
Roger tries to snatch a purse and falls.

00:07:13.536 --> 00:07:16.544
Mrs Jones responds firmly and takes him home.

00:07:16.764 --> 00:07:20.071
An open door gives him the chance to run, but he stays.

00:07:20.291 --> 00:07:24.493
She trusts him, feeds him, and avoids questions that might embarrass him.

00:07:24.713 --> 00:07:29.023
Finally, she gives him money for the shoes and tells him not to steal again.

00:07:29.243 --> 00:07:39.972
Now remember the language: snatch, grab suddenly; tug, sharp pull; suede, soft brushed leather; presentable, neat-looking; mistrusted, doubted; barely, only just.

00:07:40.192 --> 00:07:43.136
Use those exact chapter words in your own retelling.

00:07:43.356 --> 00:07:49.863
Which detail best shows Roger beginning to change: the open door, the unattended purse, or the ten dollars?

00:07:50.363 --> 00:07:51.664
Explain your choice.

00:07:59.164 --> 00:08:05.585
The open door and unattended purse both show his choice to stay and earn trust before he receives the money.

00:08:05.805 --> 00:08:09.432
An answer should explain what he does, not only name an object.

00:08:09.652 --> 00:08:11.124
You have finished the revision.

00:08:11.344 --> 00:08:17.637
Pause here and retell the whole chapter aloud, from the broken purse strap to Roger’s difficult goodbye.

00:08:17.857 --> 00:08:24.663
For an exam answer, support an idea about Mrs Jones or Roger with a precise action from the story.
